## The six use cases

Ranked by how soon each can deliver value.

| # | Use case | Site | The need today | Where the value comes from | Grounded in | Technology |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Document finder | All three | A file can sit in any of three sites and dozens of folders, so people ask a colleague or rebuild work that already exists | Minutes on every lookup, and less duplicated work | Every library in the hub, scoped by the Library, Category and Technology tags | M365 Copilot over the hub search scope |
| 2 | Development process assistant | Internal, External | Stage gate and handoff knowledge sits with a few experienced people who are interrupted constantly. The process map and RASCI exist but are rarely opened | Answers without interrupting an expert, faster onboarding, fewer reworks at stage gates | Development Process Database: process maps, RASCI, stage gate checklists, templates | A SharePoint agent scoped to one library |
| 3 | Meeting and decision follow-up | All three | Actions and decisions from CET, board and committee reviews live in individual notes | Nothing dropped between meetings | Teams meetings and chats. No SharePoint content at all | Copilot in Teams |
| 4 | Customer request support | Renewable Solutions | Responding to a customer request means pulling product, program and prior proposal material together for each opportunity | Faster turnaround on customer requests, and consistent answers across the team | Sales and Customer Requests libraries, plus approved product and program material | A SharePoint agent scoped to the sales libraries |
| 5 | Partner self-service | External | Partners need documents and process answers that already sit on the External site | Partners find answers themselves, and less staff time spent relaying documents | External site libraries only, nothing internal | A SharePoint agent scoped to the External site |
| 6 | Draft from approved templates | Internal, Renewable Solutions | Recurring documents such as SUP applications, RFP bids and decommissioning plans start from a blank page or whatever file was handy | Hours per first draft, and fewer review cycles | Approved templates plus prior approved documents in the same Category | Copilot in Word over an approved template set |
| 7 | Contract and RFP intelligence | Internal | Reviewing a contract or bid means reading across versions, prior agreements and related correspondence | Less time assembling the picture before a review, and a fuller record of what was agreed before | Contracts, proposals, negotiation history and award material | Copilot Studio agent |
| 8 | Project status briefing | Internal, Renewable Solutions | A status update means pulling from the PSF Dashboard, project trackers, decks and people | Hours per briefing, on every project, every cycle | Project trackers, schedules, handoff records and approved reports. None of this is structured today | Copilot Studio agent over a structured project list |
| 9 | Permitting and community research | Internal | Requirements, prior cases and public sentiment for a new site are gathered from many sources each time | Less time assembling research per site, and a more consistent starting picture | Permit records, ordinances and regulatory filings, plus approved external sources | Copilot Studio agent with external connectors |

Rows 1 to 3 and 6 describe work we have observed directly. Rows 4, 5, 7, 8 and 9 are our reading of how the work is done today, and each is worth confirming with the team that owns it.

Only 7, 8 and 9 justify Copilot Studio, and 9 sits largely outside SharePoint. Treating all nine as Copilot Studio work would roughly triple the estimate for the same capability.

## Phasing

Each phase unlocks the next. Content readiness sets the order.

| Phase | Use cases | Build effort | Unlocked by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1. This quarter, on the pilot sites | 1 to 3 | None to low. No code | The hub, the four tags, and clean permissions on the pilot sites |
| 2. After migration | 4 to 7 | Low to moderate | Content tagged on the way in, a way to mark the approved version, and a partner permission review before 5 |
| 3. Needs new foundations | 8, 9 | High. The data has to be built first | Project data held somewhere queryable, and a reviewed set of external sources |

Phase 3 is a data project before it is an AI project. Without the underlying data in place, an agent gives confident answers that are often wrong.

## What has to be true first

Six decisions gate the roadmap. Three are already in front of the Working Group for other reasons.

| Decision | Blocks | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| How archived content is treated | 1, 6, 7 | Open. Archive in place keeps old documents visible to AI, the main cause of confidently outdated answers |
| How the approved or current version is marked | 6, 7 | Not yet raised. Needs a Status value beyond Active and Archive |
| Permission cleanup on the pilot sites | 1, 2, 4 | In progress through the reorg |
| What partners may reach through an agent | 5 | Not yet raised. An agent on External inherits whatever that site exposes |
| Where project data will live | 8 | Not yet raised. Today it lives in decks and trackers |
| Which external sources are approved | 9 | Not yet raised. Needs legal and compliance review |
